DTSTART:20260923T130000Z
DTEND:20260923T150000Z
SUMMARY:Delimitation after 2026: Electoral Equality or Federal Balance?
LOCATION:Conference Room I\, IIC main\, India International Centre\, 40 M
 ax Mueller Marg\, Lodhi Gardens\, New Delhi 110003
URL:https://iicdelhi.in/programmes/delimitation-after-2026-electoral-equa
 lity-or-federal-balance
DESCRIPTION:Moderator: Dr. Amitabh Kundu\, Professor Emeritus at L. J. Un
 iversity Ahmedabad.\nDiscussants: Dr Yogendra Yadav\, Author\, Activist a
 nd Public Intellectual\; Prof Santosh Mehrotra\, Senior Fellow Nehru Memo
 rial Museum and Library\; Dr S. Y. Quraishi\, Former Chief Election Commi
 ssioner of India\nThe proposed delimitation of parliamentary constituenci
 es after 2026 raises complex questions\, the discussion examines the impl
 ications for states that have successfully managed population growth and 
 the challenge of balancing the democratic principle of ‘one person\, on
 e vote’ with regional equality within India’s federal system. Explori
 ng a possible middle path of allocating incremental parliamentary seats b
 ased on contemporary population data without reducing the existing repres
 entation of states.\n\nhttps://iicdelhi.in/programmes/delimitation-after-
 2026-electoral-equality-or-federal-balance
CATEGORIES:talk
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:14d64e763d5cba3d@delhi-events
DTSTAMP:20260920T105600Z
DTSTART:20260923T133000Z
DTEND:20260923T153000Z
SUMMARY:Visthapana
LOCATION:Gulmohar\, India Habitat Centre\, Lodhi Road\, New Delhi 110003
URL:https://indiahabitat.org/Events_details/1418
DESCRIPTION:Visthapana (2026/Hindi\, English/46 mins) Dirs. Neha Dixit & 
 Ashhar Farooqui . This film traces the history of the Satpura voluntary r
 elocation model\, one of the most successful instances of forest-village 
 relocation in India. Through slow\, thoughtful conversations with the ers
 twhile forest-folk as well as the officials who oversaw the relocation pr
 oject itself\, the film examines the pros and cons\, highlights and risks
  of such large projects – and how they affect the landscape\, its wildl
 ife and its human custodians. Curated by Aanchal Kapur . Collab. Global W
 ildlife Fair .\n\nhttps://indiahabitat.org/Events_details/1418
CATEGORIES:film\,cinema\,nature
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1e71e18f7862cdff@delhi-events
DTSTAMP:20260920T105600Z
DTSTART:20260923T133000Z
DTEND:20260923T153000Z
SUMMARY:IHC Theatre Festival 2026. Window Tak Aashiqui
LOCATION:The Stein Auditorium\, India Habitat Centre\, Lodhi Road\, New D
 elhi 110003
URL:https://indiahabitat.org/Events_details/1417
DESCRIPTION:IHC Theatre Festival 2026 . Window Tak Aashiqui (Hindi\, Engl
 ish/90 mins) Written & Performed by Manurishi Chadha . Set against the no
 stalgic backdrop of Old Delhi\, the play captures the innocence\, longing
 \, and charm of first love. Monu reminisces about his childhood love—a 
 love built through fleeting glances\, silent conversations\, cassette son
 gs\, and a single open window. Rooted in the old-world culture of “wind
 ow wala romance”\, the play evokes the bittersweet emotions of youthful
  love-never fully expressed\, yet unforgettable. Prod. Kathabaaz Films . 
 Tickets at Rs.200\, Rs.350\, & Rs.500 are available on bookmyshow.com and
  at the Habitat Programmes desk. Late entry is strictly prohibited .\n\nh
 ttps://indiahabitat.org/Events_details/1417
CATEGORIES:performance\,theatre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:b3329bf294ad2231@delhi-events
DTSTAMP:20260920T105600Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260924
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260929
SUMMARY:Main Hoon Warli
LOCATION:Bikaner House\, Pandara Road\, India Gate\, New Delhi 110011
URL:https://bikanerhouse.rajasthan.gov.in/upcoming-events/2026/9
DESCRIPTION:The solo exhibition of Warli paintings by acclaimed artist Sa
 njay Parhad seeks to illuminate the timeless visual language of Warli art
 —one of India’s most vital tribal art forms—by tracing its deep-roo
 ted cultural ethos and its evolving contemporary resonance. Sanjay’s wo
 rk vividly portrays the oral histories\, customs\, and cosmologies of the
  Warli community in Maharashtra. His paintings draw from the tribal commu
 nity’s collective memory\, rendering sacred myths and everyday scenes w
 ith poetic rhythm and meticulous detail. While rooted in tradition\, Parh
 ad’s practice has expanded to engage with contemporary themes\, transfo
 rming Warli art into a living\, breathing narrative of the present. Known
  for his signature linear style\, fluid motion\, and masterful use of sca
 le and space\, Parhad bridges generations of storytelling—evoking ances
 tral spirits and ecological harmony\, while commenting on modern realitie
 s. This exhibition is curated by Chhavi Jain.\n\nhttps://bikanerhouse.raj
 asthan.gov.in/upcoming-events/2026/9
CATEGORIES:exhibition\,art
END:VEVENT
