SUMMARY:Understanding Kutiyattam: A Dialogue with the Experts
LOCATION:The Theatre\, India Habitat Centre\, Lodhi Road\, New Delhi 1100
 03
URL:https://indiahabitat.org/Events_details/1447
DESCRIPTION:This session brings together distinguished practitioners and 
 experts who have dedicated their lives to the art form — Sooraj Nambiar
  \, Kutiyattam Director and Actor\; Kapila Venu \, Kutiyattam exponent\; 
 Kalamandalam Rajeev \, Mizhavu Player\; and Kalanilayam Unnikrishnan \, I
 daykka Artiste. For registrations and details\, write to habitatprogramme
 s@gmail.com\n\nhttps://indiahabitat.org/Events_details/1447
CATEGORIES:workshop
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cbe66b5f628c6171@delhi-events
DTSTAMP:20260920T105600Z
DTSTART:20260925T130000Z
DTEND:20260925T150000Z
SUMMARY:The Infrastructure of Chinese Civilizationism
LOCATION:Seminar Rooms 1\,2\,3\, India International Centre\, 40 Max Muel
 ler Marg\, Lodhi Gardens\, New Delhi 110003
URL:https://iicdelhi.in/programmes/infrastructure-chinese-civilizationism
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Prasenjit Duara\, Professor Emeritus\, Oscar Tang Di
 stinguished Chair of East Asian Studies at Duke University. Formerly\, Ch
 airman of the History Dept at University of Chicago and Raffles Professor
  and Director of the Asia Research Institute at the National University o
 f Singapore.\nChair: Amb Shivshankar Menon\, former NSA and Foreign Secre
 tary\nThe Civilizationisms project is a global collaboration that examine
 s how contemporary states use the idea of civilization for political legi
 timation. The talk analyses the rise of Chinese civilizationism as a mode
  of global power that seeks not only to challenge Western dominance but t
 o reshape the world order through infrastructure. It traces this developm
 ent through ecological civilization\, the Belt and Road Initiative\, and 
 the expansion of Chinese digital and financial networks across the Global
  South. These initiatives operate as an “infrastructure of civilization
 \,” embedding norms\, standards and digital dependencies into governanc
 e and economic life\, reflecting a broader transformation in global power
  towards infrastructural integration.\n\nhttps://iicdelhi.in/programmes/i
 nfrastructure-chinese-civilizationism
CATEGORIES:talk
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:285b2021f3b2d36a@delhi-events
DTSTAMP:20260920T105600Z
DTSTART:20260925T130000Z
DTEND:20260925T150000Z
SUMMARY:Oslo: A Tail of Promise
LOCATION:Lecture Room I\, IIC Annexe\, India International Centre\, 40 Ma
 x Mueller Marg\, Lodhi Gardens\, New Delhi 110003
URL:https://iicdelhi.in/programmes/film-oslo-tail-promise
DESCRIPTION:(2025\; 86 mins.\; English and Marathi with English subtitles
 \; Documentary)\nDirector: Isha Pungaliya\nWith Pooja R. Bhale and Oslo K
 hanwalkar\nProducers: John Abraham\, Minnakshi Das and Amey Gosavi\nA mov
 ing true story about the bond between Pooja and Oslo\, a Siberian Husky w
 ith a troubled past. Set against a journey from Pune to the Himalayas\, t
 he film weaves together experience of longing\, loss\, grief\, love\,and 
 surrender\, exploring how animals and nature can become sources of compan
 ionship.\n\nhttps://iicdelhi.in/programmes/film-oslo-tail-promise
CATEGORIES:film\,cinema
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:88a995a297bbe8a4@delhi-events
DTSTAMP:20260920T105600Z
DTSTART:20260925T133000Z
DTEND:20260925T153000Z
SUMMARY:IHC Theatre Festival 2026. Har Marz Ki Dawa
LOCATION:The Stein Auditorium\, India Habitat Centre\, Lodhi Road\, New D
 elhi 110003
URL:https://indiahabitat.org/Events_details/1421
DESCRIPTION:IHC Theatre Festival 2026 . Har Marz Ki Dawa (Hindi/75 mins).
  Wri. Moliere . Directed & Adapted by Saurabh Nayyar . Cast. Rana Pratap 
 Senger\, Preeti Rao\, Pubali Sanyal\, Kanak Khanna\, Navendra Mishra \, a
 nd others. A hilarious adaptation of Molière's classic comedy The Doctor
  in Spite of Himself . When a vengeful wife tricks everyone into believin
 g that her drunken barber husband is a miracle doctor\, chaos ensues as h
 e is drawn into a series of increasingly absurd situations\, proving that
  deception can sometimes be the best medicine. Prod. Jubilee Theatre Foun
 dation . Tickets at Rs.200\, Rs.350 & Rs.500 are available on bookmyshow.
 com and at the Habitat Programmes desk. Late entry is strictly prohibited
  .\n\nhttps://indiahabitat.org/Events_details/1421
CATEGORIES:performance\,theatre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:0d469fb1c0d7a618@delhi-events
DTSTAMP:20260920T105600Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260926
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260927
SUMMARY:The Language of Belonging
LOCATION:KNMA\, New Delhi\, Kiran Nadar Museum of Art\, Saket\, New Delhi
  110017
URL:https://www.knma.org/whats-on/events/the-language-of-belonging
DESCRIPTION:As part of KNMA’s celebration of International Week of Deaf
  People\, this panel brings together diverse Deaf perspectives to explore
  the relationship between sign language\, Deaf culture\, communication\, 
 identity\, and belonging. The conversation considers how these themes int
 ersect with art and creative spaces\, and how museums and cultural instit
 utions can foster greater inclusion and participation for Deaf and hearin
 g communities alike. Moderated by Hardeep Singh \, the panel brings toget
 her Sunil Sahasrabudhe \, Ranveet Kau r\, Harshali Nade and Niharika Popl
 i . Together\, they share perspectives on Deaf culture\, language\, acces
 sibility\, and ways of creating spaces that support meaningful communicat
 ion and participation. Following the panel\, a screening of If I Could Te
 ll You \, a documentary by Niharika Popli \, explores the experiences of 
 Deaf individuals through themes of language\, identity\, communication\, 
 and the human need to express and be understood. Following Charu\, a youn
 g Deaf woman\, alongside a hearing CODA (Child of Deaf Adults) and four y
 oung artists\, the film considers different forms of visual and performat
 ive expression. About the Contributors Hardeep Singh is a Deaf artist\, d
 esigner\, and inclusion professional at KNMA\, working to advance accessi
 bility and Deaf inclusion in museum spaces. Sunil Sahasrabudhe is Preside
 nt of the All India Federation of the Deaf (AIFD) and a leader\, educator
 \, and advocate for the Deaf community in India. Ranveet Kaur is a Deaf c
 ommunity leader\, Coordinator at the Speaking Hands Institute for the Dea
 f\, and President of The Punjab Association of Deaf. Harshali Nade is an 
 Indian Sign Language translator and communication professional with exper
 ience across news\, museums\, media\, and accessibility. She works to mak
 e information more accessible to the Deaf community through ISL translati
 on. Niharika Popli is a filmmaker\, editor\, and co-founder of Dilmaya Fi
 lms. Her work explores language\, identity\, and belonging. Her debut doc
 umentary\, Rasan Piya\, received the Tareque Masud Award at Film Southasi
 a and a Special Mention at the Mumbai International Film Festival.\n\nhtt
 ps://www.knma.org/whats-on/events/the-language-of-belonging
CATEGORIES:talk\,art\,cinema\,sociology
END:VEVENT
